  "notes": "Swedish-produced Eurodance/ragga-house with a chanted male lead, produced in the Cheiron orbit by Denniz PoP (who is entried in `pop-2000s`, not here). The ragga half is what moves the numbers: \"ragga-derived syncopated percussion and a timbale or tom fill into the chorus\" is a timbale/conga/tom perc bed running 3-7 hits a bar on the offbeat, and the bass carries \"a dancehall-derived accent pattern that leaves beat 1 light\" — an independent riff at 0.62 roots with a 0.55 anticipation so the weight falls after the downbeat rather than on it. The vocal is the other identity: \"a chanted, ragga-inflected male toast across verse and chorus, with a gang-chant answer rather than a separate diva topline\", so the melody is a low, dense, stepwise, staccato toast at 55-74 and the counter is an answering gang lick rather than an octave double. ⚠ The research marks the harmonic reading `[LOW-CONF]` (a reggae-derived offbeat keyboard chord and a bright chorus lift) and calls the drum, tempo and harmonic detail genre-derived; both marks are repeated here. ⚠ All densities, velocities and probabilities above are INFERRED from those adjectives. ⚠ **The countermelody reached 964/1000 distinct answers against a floor of 980 (2026-08-17, counter variety floor).** The 1-bar value came out of `melody.phraseBars`, which is now [2,4] weighted [4,3]. `generators::melody` writes a phrase's rhythm **once** and reuses it for every repeat, so a 1-bar phrase gives the whole clip one bar of rhythmic entropy — and the counter, whose onsets are read off the lead's, inherits that ceiling. Distinctness is combinatorial in the shape LENGTH, not in the option count. ⚠ The value that moved here is INFERRED rather than a researched claim — the entry's Melody/chords line reads \"organ/saw stabs plus a whistle- or steel-pan-ish lead riff; loops are 4 bars. The vocal is a chanted, ragga-inflected male toast across verse and chorus, with a gang-chant answer rather than a separate diva topline. Produced in the Cheiron orbit by Denniz PoP.\", and every claim in it still holds after the change.",
